WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.530
so I told you last week, or maybe it was the
week before that when corporate media makes

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:10.170
a big deal out of these foe in PR, inspirational
stories about, you know, Jimmy did a fundraiser

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:15.900
for his motorized wheelchair and his classmates
came through for him.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.600
These really are not inspirational stories.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:24.760
These are really primarily about a broken
dystopian system that forced little Jimmy

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:29.840
to need to do a bake sale for a medical device,
which then led to people who understandably

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.170
felt so horribly for Jimmy that they gave
Jimmy some of their money.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.739
And these stories really have to stop and
fair, which is fairness and accuracy in reporting

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:41.440
that are really good piece about these bogus
stories.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.469
They call them perseverance porn, which is
really a great name for them.

00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:46.679
So here are some examples.

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:50.140
Two year old Logan Moore needs a walker.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.249
Insurance wouldn't cover it.

00:00:52.249 --> 00:00:54.710
Home Depot built one from parts.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.960
Fox five DC proclaimed, this is awesome.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:04.460
On Good Morning America donating vacation
time to new moms is a trendy coworker, baby

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:05.650
shower gift.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.330
How lovely, wrong.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:08.330
It's not lovely.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:09.330
Okay?

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.180
This is a terrible system that doesn't give
new moms paid time off.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.970
They can't afford paid time off and we've
not decided as a society that this is something

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.730
that we're going to take care of.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.721
There's a whole bunch of other ones that we're
putting up on the screen about a 12 year old

00:01:22.721 --> 00:01:29.170
boy who raked leaves to afford a gravestone
for his friend who died of cancer, a 13 year

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:34.000
old boy who sold his x-box and did yard work
to buy his single mom a car so she could get

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.090
to work.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.310
These are uplifting stories of individual
will in a sense like there's no doubt about

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:40.310
that.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:45.930
I'm not denying that, but what they really
tell us that is notable is how truly dystopian

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.480
our economic reality has become in the United
